      "modelAnswer": "Yunus's point of view is that poverty in Bangladesh is not the fault of the poor but of institutions that fail to serve them; he believes the poor—including destitute women—deserve bank credit and that microfinance institutions like his Grameen Bank can lift people out of poverty.",
      "explanation": "Full credit (1 point) for correctly identifying Yunus's point of view. Acceptable responses (NYSED rating guide) include: the Bangladeshi people could emerge from poverty with a microfinance institution; the people could emerge from poverty; Bangladesh was challenged by financial apartheid; current institutions failed to serve the poor; destitute women have not been given enough chances to borrow; the poor deserve bank credit/loans; poverty can be lessened by Grameen Bank; Grameen Bank can help people cross the poverty line; there is extreme poverty in Bangladesh; loaning to the poor is a good investment for ending poverty; or he believes in creating a world without poverty. Not acceptable: 'he blames the poor,' 'more have crossed the poverty line,' 'there are 7 million poor people,' 'the money you lend will never come back,' 'people are wasting their money,' 'banks should not help the poor,' or 'he has eliminated poverty in the world.'",
